  "title": "Week 8: Tartu",
  "date": "2026-02-26T22:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "Week 8: Tartu\n\nJust before my Museum Card expired, I decided to set foot in Tartu! 🚆 Known as the second-largest city in Estonia, it is truly the country's intellectual and cultural heartbeat. It’s home to the prestigious University of Tartu and the stunning National Museum. 🏛️✨\n\nTo get there from Tallinn, you can choose between the train or the bus. 🚌 Pro tip: Buses are much cheaper and offer way more time slots than the train! 💸\n\n🏛️ Estonian National Museum (Eesti Rahva Muuseum)\n\nI left Tallinn in the morning and arrived in Tartu just in time for lunch after a smooth 2-hour ride. 🥪 The National Museum is located in the suburbs, about 3 km from the city center. Right in front of it, you’ll find the famous Upside Down House (Tagurpidi Maja)! 🏠🙃\n\nTagurpidi Maja\n\nWhile the collection itself is niche, the museum experience is top-notch. 🎫 You can swipe your ticket at any exhibit to save the information online to read later—super high-tech! 💻 Check out my digital collection here: My ERM Link.\n\nTicket\n\n🍻 Gunpowder Cellar (Püssirohukelder)\n\nThis unique spot was originally an 18th-century gunpowder storage hall built into the hillside near the University. 💣 Today, it has been converted into a massive restaurant with incredibly high ceilings! 🥘\n\nPüssirohukelder\n\n⛸️ City Hall (Raekoda)\n\nThe vibe at the City Hall square is magical. ❄️ There is a free skating ground set up right in front! If you don't have your own skates, you can rent a pair for just 8 euros. ⛸️✨\n\nCity hall\n\n🏫 University of Tartu Museum (Tartu Ülikooli muuseum)\n\nLocated on Toome Hill, this building is a fascinating mix of a restored museum and ancient ruins. 🧱 It was largely destroyed during the Great Northern War, but now it serves as the university's museum, offering a deep dive into Estonian history from 1300 to 1900. 📜\n\nUniversity Museum\n\n💭 Final Thoughts\n\nTartu feels very different from Tallinn. It doesn't rely on a \"Medieval Old Town\" vibe; instead, the entire city feels like one big, academic campus. 🎓\n\nAlso, a fun observation: unlike Tallinn, where Rimi or Selver seem to be everywhere, Coop is the king of the streets here. 🛒 And honestly? The fried chicken at Coop is surprisingly good. 🍗😋",
